 "observations": [
  "The page number «179» stands at the head of the page, centred, with a rule below it; at the right the number of the table, «Tab. 65», in bold sans serif, underlined. At the foot, the signature «23*» on the right; recorded here, not transcribed as a line. The titles (L01-L02), as on p. 178 (JP2 0210), «Gedval elbuht (O)» and «Tabula motus Solis et Lunae diurni et horarii atque momentanei (N)», in bold, the sigla in roman; a speck above the u of «elbuht» and a speck after the 2 of the second column in L36; not transcribed. The folios of the manuscripts stand at the right of the second title in two lines, «C 78v, O 135v,» and «N 40v.», the letters raised; here as right margin notes.",
  "The table: as on p. 178, with notes 1-3 in the heads. Here each head is set from the top, one printed line to a line (L03-L07); the cells separated by our «|»; a head followed by empty cells spans their columns. The range of note d is marked at its first and last rows, 138° and 150° (L25, L37).",
  "L08-L37. The arguments, the anomalies 4s 1° to 5s 0° and their complements 7s 29° to 7s 0°, with N's reading of note a at 5s 0°. Checked: the law 2′ 28″ − 5.8″ cos α of p. 178 gives the second column within 0.6″, the sixth (five times) within 0.9″ and the fourth (6.6 times) within 2.1″; the fourth column stays at 16′ 49″ from 142° to 146° and at 16′ 50″ from 147° to 150°.",
  "L08-L37, continued. The moon: the seventh column is 1 3/5 times the third within 1.0″ and the fifth 0.4846 times the third within 1.0″ in every row, as on pp. 175-176. The third column rises by 2″ or 3″ a degree to 135° and by 1″ or 2″ from there to 35′ 20″ at 150°; it lies above 32′ 56″ 27‴ − 2′ 43.9″ cos α by 1.0″ to 7.7″, most at 134°-135°.",
  "Notes on the readings. Checked: O's 35′ 17″ at 148° (note b, against C's 16) keeps the steps of the third column, 1″ and 1″, and agrees with the seventh column divided by 1 3/5, 35′ 16.9″, and the fifth divided by 0.4846, 35′ 17.2″; C's 16′ 38″ at 123° (note c, against O's 37) is 6.6 times the law of the sun, 16′ 37.6″; C's 16′ 46″ at 125° (note e, against O's 47) is the nearer to 0.4846 times the third column, 16′ 45.1″; C's 55′ 44″ at 131° (note f, against O's 43) is 1 3/5 times the third column, 55′ 44.0″. The minutes 17 of the radius of the moon from 138° to 150° (notes d-d, O, against C's 16) complete the 16′ 59″ of 137°. Note a: N's 5, against the 4 of C and O, makes the argument of L37 5s 0°, 150°, the complement of 7s 0°.",
  "Notes 1-3: as on p. 178. Italics: «Hos titulos om.». N01: the ellipses «. . . .» of the lemmas as printed, the first set close. N03: «{sup:d-d})», the two letters of the range joined by a hyphen, raised, before one parenthesis."
